{"chunk_id":"6b826f","wall_time_seconds":0.000003334,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":553,"output":"### Basic WeakReference Usage

Source: https://www.php.net/manual/en/class.weakreference.php

Demonstrates creating a weak reference to an object and verifying that it returns null after the original object is unset.

```php
<?php

$obj = new stdClass();
$weakref = WeakReference::create($obj);

var_dump($weakref->get());

unset($obj);

var_dump($weakref->get());

?>
```

--------------------------------

### WeakReference::get

Source: https://www.php.net/manual/en/class.weakreference.php

Retrieves the object that is being weakly referenced.

```APIDOC
## WeakReference::get()

### Description
Returns the object that is being weakly referenced. If the original object has been destroyed, this method returns null.

### Returns
- **?object** - The referenced object or null if the object has been destroyed.
```

--------------------------------

### WeakReference::create

Source: https://www.php.net/manual/en/class.weakreference.php

Creates a new weak reference to the specified object.

```APIDOC
## WeakReference::create(object $object)

### Description
Creates a new weak reference to the provided object.

### Parameters
- **object** (object) - The object to be weakly referenced.

### Returns
- **WeakReference** - A new instance of WeakReference.
```

--------------------------------

### Basic WeakMap usage

Source: https://www.php.net/manual/en/class.weakmap.php

Demonstrates how an object used as a key in a WeakMap is garbage collected once all other references to it are removed.

```php
<?php
$wm = new WeakMap();

$o = new stdClass;

class A {
    public function __destruct() {
        echo "Dead!\n";
    }
}

$wm[$o] = new A;

var_dump(count($wm));
echo "Unsetting...\n";
unset($o);
echo "Done\n";
var_dump(count($wm));
```

### The WeakReference class > Introduction

Source: https://www.php.net/manual/en/class.weakreference.php

Weak references allow a programmer to maintain a reference to an object without preventing that object from being destroyed. This mechanism is particularly useful for implementing cache-like structures. Because creating a weak reference does not increase the object's reference count, the object is destroyed once its reference count drops to zero.
"}

